    "haunt": {
      "word": "HAUNT",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "To frequent; to resort to frequently; to visit pertinaciously or intrusively; to intrude upon. You wrong me, sir, thus still to haunt my house. Shak. Those cares that haunt the court and town. Swift.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To inhabit or frequent as a specter; to visit as a ghost or apparition. Foul spirits haunt my resting place. Fairfax.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To practice; to devote one's self to. [Obs.] That other merchandise that men haunt with fraud . . . is cursed. Chaucer. Leave honest pleasure, and haunt no good pastime. Ascham.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To accustom; to habituate. [Obs.] Haunt thyself to pity. Wyclif.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To persist in staying or visiting. I've charged thee not to haunt about my doors. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "A place to which one frequently resorts; as, drinking saloons are the haunts of tipplers; a den is the haunt of wild beasts. in his business was called a haunt. The household nook, The haunt of all affections pure. Keble. The feeble soul, a haunt of fears. Tennyson.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The habit of resorting to a place. [Obs.] The haunt you have got about the courts. Arbuthnot.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Practice; skill. [Obs.] Of clothmaking she hadde such an haunt. Chaucer.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        }
      ],
